timestamp=2026-11-04T19:38:15.308Z level=INFO run=5b2c05d3 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Try a more principled approach: detect changepoints in each sensor's time series\n# Physical onset is when state first began to deviate - this is like the start of a change\n# We need to correct for response delay: observed = physical + delay? Or observed reflects physical state as of T - delay\n# \"the observed reading at time T reflects the physical state as of time T minus the response delay\"\n# So physical_state(T-δ) causes observed_reading(T). That means if the physical state changed at time t_phys, it appears in observed readings at t_obs = t_phys + δ. So to get t_phys, t_phys = t_obs - δ. Which is what we did.\n\n# But the key is \"first began to deviate from normal operating conditions\" - this could be very subtle (trend change), not just crossing threshold.\n# Let us use change point detection (e.g. ruptures or simple CUSUM) on each sensor, then get the earliest t_phys per subsystem.\n\n# Let us implement a simple CUSUM\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cusum_onset(ts, vals, min_size=50):\n    # CUSUM for upward/downward shift\n    mu = np.mean(vals[:min_size])\n    std = np.std(vals[:min_size]) + 1e-10\n    if std == 0:\n        return -1\n    cp = -1\n    # try to detect when it starts drifting\n    s_pos = 0\n    s_neg = 0\n    for i in range(min_size, len(vals)):\n        s_pos = max(0, s_pos + (vals[i] - mu)/std)\n        s_neg = max(0, s_neg - (vals[i] - mu)/std)\n        if s_pos > 5 or s_neg > 5:  # threshold\n            cp = i\n            break\n    return cp\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
