timestamp=2026-11-04T19:38:38.874Z level=INFO run=5b2c05d3 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Try another detector: Bayesian/structural break - simpler: PELT-like idea or just detect when mean shifts persistently\n# Or look at the problem more: \"physical state first began to deviate from normal operating conditions\"\n# \"Normal operating conditions\" are defined by normal_min/normal_max ranges. So deviation from normal means the state goes outside the normal band?\n# But \"began to deviate\" is a start. Also \"not when sensors detected the deviation\" - detection is when reading crossed threshold or alarmed. And observed = physical_state_at(T-δ), so the physical state crossed the threshold at t_phys = t_obs_breach - δ. That matches what we first computed.\n# But that's when it crossed the threshold. Is there deviation before crossing threshold? Maybe the state was trending towards abnormal but still within bounds? That is still a deviation from steady normal.\n# That depends on interpretation. But \"normal operating conditions\" are typically the normal range. Also \"onset time is when its physical state first began to deviate from normal...\" - deviation as in moving away from the normal regime.\n# Also look at the full event timing. The answer has 5 subsystems - let us think about typical PWR progression. Turbine/generator issues can trigger cascade; or steam generator; or core; or loss of cooling. The earliest physical onsets we saw with threshold-crossing (corrected for delay): TURBINE at 16:58 on 15th (TURB_OUTPUT drops), STEAM_GEN at 21:59 (STEAM_PRES high/low?), COOLING at 00:09 on 16th (pressure), PRIMARY_LOOP at 04:32, CORE at 09:36. That order fits a propagation.\n# Also alarms start coming in: STEAM_GEN alarm at 20:00, TURBINE at 18:00 - close after physical onset. COOLING alarm at 00:10 - almost immediately after phys onset (phys 00:09). Operator actions: emergency shutdown on TURBINE at 02:52 (16th), increase cooling at 03:35, adjust feedwater at 05:08, reduce power at 08:12, scram at 15:56. The propagation order suggested by actions and alarms happening in sequence is TURBINE first, then COOLING/STEAM actions follow... and the sequence is: TURBINE action first (early), then COOLING, then STEAM_GEN, then PRIMARY_LOOP, then CORE (SCRAM very late). That matches: TURBINE (first), STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE. That is consistent.\n# Also physically, turbine trip can cause pressure transients in steam generator and primary loop; loss of heat removal affects core. That common sequence is often TURBINE/SG issues leading to primary/cooling disturbances, then core. Or depending. Another common: loss of coolant -> primary -> core; but cooling subsystem is involved. Let us look at the data more.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
