timestamp=2026-11-04T19:39:48.305Z level=INFO run=5b2c05d3 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Let us step back. The task is very specific: \"determine the full causal propagation order — rank all five subsystems from the one whose physical state first began deviating from normal operating conditions to the one that was affected last.\"\n\n# \"Propagation through physical connections\" means a failure in one subsystem propagates to connected subsystems. In a reactor, common causal chains:\n# - Turbine trip -> sudden change in steam demand -> affects steam generator -> affects primary loop (temperature/pressure) -> affects core (cooling)\n# - Or loss of coolant accident affecting primary and core directly?\n# - Or steam generator tube rupture affecting primary loop, etc.\n\n# Also looking at the earliest threshold breaches (phys times): TURBINE output at 16:58 (15th), STEAM_GEN pressure at 21:59 (15th), COOLING pressure at 00:09 (16th), PRIMARY_LOOP temp at 04:32 (16th), CORE exit at 09:36 (16th). That gives order TURBINE -> STEAM_GEN -> COOLING -> PRIMARY_LOOP -> CORE.\n# That fits typical turbine trip leading to transient that cascades. Also alarms: TURBINE alarms early (~18:00), then STEAM_GEN, then COOLING, etc. Operator actions start with TURBINE emergency shutdown at 02:52 - treating TURBINE as the problem area? Or reacting to it.\n# Also the very first sensor (any sensor) to show physical deviation at all - earliest t_phys across all sensors? Let us check.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
